  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36406v1</id>
    <title>Adaptive Calibration of RF Digital Twins via Budget-Constrained Radio Measurement Selection</title>
    <updated>2026-10-26T23:40:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36406v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.36406v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Radio-frequency (RF) digital twins (DTs) enable site-specific wireless propagation prediction by explicitly reconstructing the physical environment and modeling electromagnetic interactions. Although physics-based DTs reduce reliance on exhaustive measurements, systematic bias relative to real radio data arises from environmental uncertainty, material inaccuracies, and geometric abstractions. Calibration using field measurements is therefore necessary but costly under limited sampling budgets. This paper studies active RF DT calibration, in which measurement locations are selected sequentially and in an online manner to estimate a calibration offset under strict measurement budget constraints. A physics-based RF DT pipeline is developed, integrating semantic geospatial data from OpenStreetMap, 3D modeling in Blender, and deterministic ray tracing in Sionna to capture dominant building and foliage effects. This RF DT is calibrated using path-loss measurements collected with software-defined radios. Multiple online calibration strategies that select radio measurement locations are compared, including random sampling, rank-structured sampling with balanced coverage, quasi-Monte Carlo ranked selection, progressive structure-aware allocation, and fully adaptive uncertainty-driven sampling with estimator stabilization. Results demonstrate that adaptive and structure-aware schemes significantly improve both mean and worst-case calibration accuracy, particularly in the low-budget regime for radio measurements.</summary>
    <category term="cs.NI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.SP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-26T23:40:54Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.NI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yalin E. Sagduyu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tugba Erpek</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hossein Jafari</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
